# Timing-measurement constraints for fpga/soc_fpga.v.
#
# This file exists to answer one question - what Fmax does this design
# actually achieve - without inventing a board. It constrains *only* the
# clock, and is meant to be used with nextpnr's --lpf-allow-unconstrained so
# every I/O is placed wherever the router likes.
#
# Why not use generic.lpf: every pin in it is a placeholder. Pinning I/O to
# fictional sites does not make a timing result more real, it makes it less -
# the router would be optimizing against a board that does not exist, and on
# a package where some of those sites may not even be I/O.
#
# What this measures: the internal logic's maximum frequency. What it does
# not: I/O timing, or the routing pressure a real pinout imposes. For a
# design whose critical path is the CPU-plus-bus (it is - see fpga/README.md),
# that is the number that matters, but it is an upper bound on a real board
# rather than a promise.
#
# 100 MHz is asked for deliberately: nextpnr optimizes against the requested
# frequency and then reports what it actually achieved, so requesting more
# than the design can do is how you find out what it can do.
FREQUENCY PORT "clk" 25 MHZ;
